WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.288 --> 00:00:14.670
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:15.151 --> 00:00:22.853
Welcome to another episode of Superstars in History, the talk show featuring exclusive interviews with people from the

3
00:00:22.953 --> 00:00:25.373
past. I'm Faye Moss.

4
00:00:26.354 --> 00:00:31.955
Today's guest never forgot her humble background when she became world famous.

5
00:00:32.895 --> 00:00:38.901
As the First Lady of Argentina, she worked tirelessly to help the working class.

6
00:00:39.982 --> 00:00:43.763
Straight from history, here is Eva Perón!

7
00:00:58.730 --> 00:00:59.450
Thank you, Faye.

8
00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:01.171
Please—call me Evita.

9
00:01:02.131 --> 00:01:04.452
To my people I was always Evita.

10
00:01:05.752 --> 00:01:07.313
Okay, Evita!

11
00:01:07.793 --> 00:01:13.794
You were born in May 1919 in the small village of Los Toldos, Argentina.

12
00:01:15.035 --> 00:01:16.475
What was your childhood like?

13
00:01:17.835 --> 00:01:21.096
I was the youngest of five children, and my mother raised us alone.

14
00:01:22.517 --> 00:01:24.657
As a child I was aware that we weren't rich.

15
00:01:25.998 --> 00:01:30.999
Argentina was a prosperous country, but privileged landowners had all the wealth.

16
00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:36.124
One time I heard a working man say that the rich were too rich.

17
00:01:38.045 --> 00:01:39.165
I never forgot that!

18
00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:43.027
Did you dream of becoming famous?

19
00:01:44.607 --> 00:01:47.128
Yes, but not through politics!

20
00:01:48.308 --> 00:01:53.990
After appearing in a school play, I dreamed of becoming an actor in Buenos Aires, which was a

21
00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:55.530
huge, glamorous city.

22
00:01:56.671 --> 00:02:00.032
At 15 I moved to Buenos Aires and began working.

23
00:02:01.788 --> 00:02:05.250
I got small parts on stage, in film, and on radio.

24
00:02:06.351 --> 00:02:09.172
Soon I became a radio soap opera star.

25
00:02:10.453 --> 00:02:13.614
My real life began when I met Perón.

26
00:02:14.955 --> 00:02:18.377
You're referring to Colonel Juan Perón!

27
00:02:18.717 --> 00:02:19.718
How did you meet?

28
00:02:21.138 --> 00:02:28.082
In January 1944 an earthquake struck the city of San Juan, and six thousand people were killed.

29
00:02:29.343 --> 00:02:34.281
Perón planned a huge gala to collect donations for the earthquake victims.

30
00:02:35.161 --> 00:02:39.823
We met there, and from then on, I was always by his side.

31
00:02:40.984 --> 00:02:48.047
Colonel Perón was a high-profile member of the military government that was ruling Argentina at the time.

32
00:02:49.087 --> 00:02:56.070
Yes. As secretary of labor, Perón gave workers a minimum wage, paid holidays, and medical care.

33
00:02:56.970 --> 00:03:01.272
Never before had an Argentine leader cared so much about workers!

34
00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:06.341
Several months after we first met, he was appointed vice president.

35
00:03:07.662 --> 00:03:14.704
Unfortunately his rapid rise created enemies, and many within the government resented Perón's popularity.

36
00:03:16.104 --> 00:03:20.605
Tell us about the turbulent events of October 1945.

37
00:03:21.766 --> 00:03:25.847
The factions against Perón pressured him to resign, and he agreed.

38
00:03:27.147 --> 00:03:34.228
But in a farewell speech, he announced wage increases for workers, which infuriated his enemies.

39
00:03:35.329 --> 00:03:37.411
A few days later, Perón was arrested.

40
00:03:38.372 --> 00:03:46.060
On October 17 huge crowds of workers peacefully marched to Casa Rosada, the seat of the government, to

41
00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:47.121
protest the arrest.

42
00:03:48.242 --> 00:03:51.284
They were called the descamisados—the shirtless ones.

43
00:03:52.606 --> 00:03:54.788
How did they get that name, Evita?

44
00:03:56.048 --> 00:04:00.831
Many marchers took off their coats, and some even their shirts, because the weather was hot!

45
00:04:02.012 --> 00:04:06.916
With over 200,000 supporters in the public square, Perón was released from jail.

46
00:04:07.896 --> 00:04:11.599
The people cheered for 15 minutes when he stepped onto the balcony!

47
00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.702
You and Perón were married the next day.

48
00:04:16.622 --> 00:04:24.007
When the military government agreed to disband and hold a free election, Juan Perón decided to run for

49
00:04:24.088 --> 00:04:28.342
president. You were highly visible during his campaign.

50
00:04:29.963 --> 00:04:31.604
Many disapproved of that!

51
00:04:32.685 --> 00:04:36.367
In Latin America, wives stayed at home and out of politics.

52
00:04:37.448 --> 00:04:43.192
But Perón and I were a team, and this continued after he was elected in February 1946.

53
00:04:44.653 --> 00:04:47.475
As first lady I met with the descamisados.

54
00:04:48.536 --> 00:04:51.998
When giving speeches I always spoke passionately, from my heart.

55
00:04:52.979 --> 00:04:54.640
They would shout, "Perón!

56
00:04:55.164 --> 00:04:57.685
Evita! Our lives for Perón!"

57
00:04:58.766 --> 00:05:01.427
I was also vocal about equal rights for women.

58
00:05:02.547 --> 00:05:09.070
I organized and became president of the Peronista Women's Party and supported a bill that allowed women to

59
00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:15.873
vote. The Argentine workers loved you and Perón, but you also had your enemies.

60
00:05:17.013 --> 00:05:18.834
The descamisados loved us.

61
00:05:19.914 --> 00:05:24.416
When they asked for 40 percent higher wages, we gave them 50 percent!

62
00:05:24.436 --> 00:05:28.780
We did a lot of good for them—shortening their work hours and building them houses.

63
00:05:29.841 --> 00:05:35.886
But the wealthy, the students, the intellectuals they hated us and called us fascists!

64
00:05:36.887 --> 00:05:38.068
This didn't deter me.

65
00:05:39.108 --> 00:05:45.694
I owned two newspapers and controlled several radio stations in Buenos Aires, so I suppressed criticism of Perón

66
00:05:45.734 --> 00:05:46.014
and me.

67
00:05:46.915 --> 00:05:52.640
Yes, we silenced our critics at home, but we did so to protect our descamisados.

68
00:05:53.896 --> 00:05:56.978
Unfortunately I couldn't stop the critics when I visited Europe.

69
00:05:58.759 --> 00:06:02.881
You're talking about your Rainbow Tour in 1947.

70
00:06:03.821 --> 00:06:07.143
You were the biggest news story all over the world!

71
00:06:08.284 --> 00:06:10.865
The media followed your every move.

72
00:06:12.026 --> 00:06:18.229
Yes, I wanted to be a rainbow—a bridge between Argentina and the countries I visited.

73
00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:23.572
I had planned to visit Britain, but the press called me the wife of a dictator!

74
00:06:24.616 --> 00:06:25.817
I canceled my visit there.

75
00:06:26.957 --> 00:06:30.819
In Spain the crowds greeted me so warmly!

76
00:06:31.759 --> 00:06:33.400
And in Italy I met the Pope.

77
00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:36.421
I also visited France and Switzerland.

78
00:06:37.461 --> 00:06:45.685
After two months I was eager to return to my three great loves: Argentina, my descamisados, and Perón.

79
00:06:46.725 --> 00:06:54.669
When you returned to Argentina, you established the Eva Perón Foundation, a charity that— No, Faye!

80
00:06:55.269 --> 00:06:56.671
This was not a charity.

81
00:06:57.792 --> 00:07:00.414
My foundation provided social justice.

82
00:07:01.495 --> 00:07:06.499
We gave the poor what we owed them so there would be fewer rich and fewer poor!

83
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:11.043
And the Argentine people were so generous; they gave voluntarily.

84
00:07:13.105 --> 00:07:16.247
Well some had to be pressured to contribute.

85
00:07:17.348 --> 00:07:21.592
We built schools, hospitals, parks, and recreation centers.

86
00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:25.961
Women and children lined up at my office to tell me their needs.

87
00:07:26.961 --> 00:07:31.763
I made sure that they received clothing, furniture, medicine, toys, and cash.

88
00:07:32.823 --> 00:07:34.243
They came to me defeated.

89
00:07:35.304 --> 00:07:37.924
I understood their suffering and injustice.

90
00:07:39.045 --> 00:07:43.846
That was why, in my speeches, I often shouted that justice would be done!

91
00:07:45.366 --> 00:07:48.587
Your work earned you the nickname The Lady of Hope.

92
00:07:49.488 --> 00:07:56.910
You were so popular that when Perón ran for reelection in 1951, the people wanted you to be

93
00:07:56.991 --> 00:07:57.871
vice president!

94
00:07:59.772 --> 00:08:03.613
No woman had ever run for such a powerful position.

95
00:08:04.494 --> 00:08:09.596
When Perón announced his candidacy, the huge crowd chanted, "Evita!

96
00:08:10.156 --> 00:08:13.017
Evita!" hoping I would join the ticket.

97
00:08:14.178 --> 00:08:16.719
I said no, but the crowd protested.

98
00:08:17.599 --> 00:08:21.781
So I asked for four days, then two, then one day to decide.

99
00:08:22.141 --> 00:08:23.902
Still they shouted, "Evita!

100
00:08:24.343 --> 00:08:31.827
Evita!" As I had not been feeling well for some time, I officially declined over a week later.

101
00:08:33.148 --> 00:08:35.089
Of course, Perón was reelected!

102
00:08:36.249 --> 00:08:40.032
Millions of women, including me, cast our first votes.

103
00:08:41.312 --> 00:08:44.234
Evita, thanks so much for joining us!

104
00:08:45.275 --> 00:08:47.436
We'll let you get back to history now.

105
00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:49.641
Thank you, Faye.

106
00:08:53.064 --> 00:08:58.488
Unknown to the public, Evita's health had been declining due to advanced cancer.

107
00:08:59.589 --> 00:09:06.895
Shortly into Juan Perón's second term, Eva Perón died on July 26, 1952.

108
00:09:07.976 --> 00:09:10.278
She was 33 years old.

109
00:09:11.419 --> 00:09:17.004
Flowers sold out in Buenos Aires as Argentina plunged into mourning.

110
00:09:18.096 --> 00:09:23.521
Over two million mourners came to see their beloved Evita lying in state.

111
00:09:24.621 --> 00:09:31.927
In an era when women had little role in politics, Evita was known around the world for her

112
00:09:31.987 --> 00:09:33.208
political activities.

113
00:09:34.289 --> 00:09:39.273
Her story eventually became immortalized in the rock opera Evita.

114
00:09:40.994 --> 00:09:42.876
I hope you enjoyed today's show.

115
00:09:43.917 --> 00:09:47.239
See you next time on Superstars in History.
